(Regression: `send` is a keyword only inside the `Send signal ...`
 construct; everywhere else it is an ordinary identifier, so a
 user-defined `send` function still compiles and runs. Both meanings
 coexist in one program: a zero-argument `send` function is defined and
 called, and a `Send signal 0 to process 999999.` statement exercises
 the error path. Mirrors the 0.3.7 fix that made `begin`/`stop`/
 `finish` contextual rather than reserved. Expected: "sent" then
 "no such process".)

To send.
  Print "sent".

send.

Send signal 0 to process 999999.
On error print "no such process".